# Pilot (first officer / JFO) interview practice. Indian airline selection usually runs:
# written test → technical interview → simulator check → HR / competency interview.
# Answers to behavioural questions are best told with STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result.
# (id, kind, question, covers separated by |)
INTERVIEW = [
# --- scenario / CRM ---
("s01","scenario","On approach, you think the captain has set the wrong altitude. What do you do?","Speak up early and clearly, using the captain's name and the fact (\"Captain, cleared 3000, 4000 is set\")|Follow the escalation ladder: query, suggest, then state firmly or take action if safety is at risk|Keep it about the error, not the person|Why it matters: most accidents involve a missed chance to speak up (CRM, assertiveness)"),
("s02","scenario","The weather at destination drops below minimums while you're holding. Fuel is getting low. Walk us through your thinking.","Know your fuel: minimum diversion fuel and final reserve|Decide before fuel decides for you: set a 'bingo' time to divert|Use a decision model like FORDEC (Facts, Options, Risks, Decision, Execution, Check)|Declare MINIMUM FUEL or MAYDAY FUEL as the rules require|Brief the cabin and passengers"),
("s03","scenario","A passenger becomes unruly and refuses to sit down during taxi. What do you do?","The cockpit door stays closed — the flight crew don't leave the flight deck|Cabin crew handle it, with the captain's authority behind them|Options: stop and return to stand, involve security|Follow company procedures and report it"),
("s04","scenario","You've finished your duty and are tired, but crew scheduling asks you to fly one more sector. What do you say?","Fatigue is a safety issue, not a weakness|Know the flight duty time limits (DGCA CAR on FDTL)|Say no politely if you're not fit, and report fatigue through the proper channel|Show you'd still help find a solution"),
("s05","scenario","During take-off roll at 80 knots you get an amber caution. What happens?","Know your airline's 'low speed' vs 'high speed' regimes and V1|Below 100 kt you'd usually reject for any failure; above it only for serious ones|Callouts and who decides (the captain calls 'STOP' or 'GO')|After V1 you continue and deal with it in the air"),
("s06","scenario","You notice your captain smells of alcohol before a flight. What do you do?","Safety first: the flight should not go with an impaired pilot|Raise it privately and respectfully first if possible|If not resolved, report to the company (duty manager / chief pilot)|Mention DGCA's pre-flight breath analyser checks"),
("s07","scenario","Tell us about a time you made a mistake in training. What did you learn?","Pick a real, small mistake — not a disaster and not a fake one|STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result|Focus on what you changed afterwards|Shows honesty and a learning mindset (just culture)"),
("s08","scenario","Halfway to destination a passenger has a medical emergency. What are your options?","Ask cabin crew for details and for a doctor on board|Consider diverting to the nearest suitable airport|Declare PAN PAN or MAYDAY as appropriate and coordinate with ATC|Weigh weather, fuel and medical facilities at the options"),
("s09","scenario","You disagree with the captain's decision but it's not unsafe. What do you do?","Voice your view once, clearly, with reasons|Accept the captain's authority if it's within safe limits|Debrief it afterwards|Shows teamwork and respect for the chain of command"),
("s10","scenario","ATC gives you a clearance you're not sure you heard correctly. What do you do?","Never guess — ask 'Say again' or read back what you think and ask for confirmation|Check with the other pilot|Why: readback errors are a known cause of altitude busts"),
("s11","scenario","Describe a stressful situation and how you handled it.","STAR format|What you felt and what you did to stay calm (breathing, checklists, prioritising)|Aviate, navigate, communicate|What the result was and what you'd do again"),
("s12","scenario","You're flying with a captain who is very quiet and doesn't brief properly. How do you work with them?","Take the initiative: ask questions and brief your own parts|Keep the flight deck gradient comfortable — not too steep, not too flat|Use standard calls to build a shared picture|Stay professional, no complaining"),
# --- technical ---
("t01","technical","What is V1?","Take-off decision speed: the highest speed at which you can still reject the take-off and stop on the runway|After V1 you must continue|Compare VR (rotation) and V2 (take-off safety speed)"),
("t02","technical","Explain the difference between IAS, CAS, TAS and groundspeed.","IAS: read off the instrument|CAS: IAS corrected for instrument and position errors|TAS: CAS corrected for density (and compressibility) — speed through the air|GS: TAS corrected for wind — speed over the ground"),
("t03","technical","What are the four forces in flight and what happens in a steady climb?","Lift, weight, thrust, drag|In a steady climb, lift is slightly less than weight; part of the weight is balanced by thrust|Climb performance depends on excess thrust (angle) and excess power (rate)"),
("t04","technical","What causes a stall, and how do you recover?","Exceeding the critical angle of attack — it can happen at any speed or attitude|Recovery: reduce angle of attack (nose down) first, then roll wings level, then power|Know stall warning signs and the effect of weight, load factor and flaps on stall speed"),
("t05","technical","What is the difference between QNH, QFE and standard pressure, and when is each used?","QNH: altitude above MSL, used below transition altitude|QFE: height above the aerodrome|1013.2 hPa: flight levels above transition|Where the transition altitude and level are"),
("t06","technical","Explain how an ILS works.","Localiser: lateral guidance, VHF 108–112 MHz|Glide path: vertical guidance, UHF 329–335 MHz, usually 3°|Markers or DME for range|Categories (CAT I, II, III) and their minima"),
("t07","technical","What is the effect of high temperature and high elevation on take-off performance?","Lower air density → less lift and less engine thrust|Longer take-off run and poorer climb|Density altitude concept|Hot-and-high airports, possible weight limits"),
("t08","technical","What is a jet stream and why does it matter to us?","Narrow band of very strong wind near the tropopause|Saves time and fuel with a tailwind, costs with a headwind|Clear air turbulence near its edges|Subtropical jet over north India in winter"),
("t09","technical","Tell us about the hazards of a thunderstorm.","Turbulence, icing, hail, lightning, microbursts and wind shear|Stay at least 20 NM away from severe storms|Never fly underneath a CB on approach|Use weather radar and ATC deviations"),
("t10","technical","What is wind shear and how would you recover from it on approach?","Sudden change in wind speed or direction|Microbursts below CBs are the most dangerous|Recovery: TOGA power, pitch to the guidance (or about 15°), don't change configuration, follow through till clear|Report it to ATC"),
("t11","technical","What is the difference between an aerodrome's TORA, TODA and ASDA?","TORA: runway length available for the ground run|TODA: TORA plus clearway|ASDA: TORA plus stopway|Used to check take-off and accelerate-stop distances"),
("t12","technical","What is RVSM and what equipment does an aircraft need for it?","Reduced Vertical Separation Minimum: 1,000 ft between FL290 and FL410|Two independent altimeters, autopilot with altitude hold, altitude alerter, Mode C/S transponder|Needs approval for the aircraft and the operator"),
# --- airline & HR ---
("a01","airline","Tell us about yourself.","About 90 seconds: who you are, your training, a highlight, why this job|Keep it professional and positive|End with why you're ready for this airline"),
("a02","airline","Why do you want to fly for us?","Name real things about the airline: fleet, network, safety record, culture|Link them to your own goals|Avoid 'because you're hiring' or salary"),
("a03","airline","Where do you see yourself in five years?","Building experience as a first officer and becoming a strong team member|Command upgrade as a longer goal|Shows loyalty and realistic ambition"),
("a04","airline","What are your strengths and weaknesses?","One real strength with a quick example|One real weakness and what you're doing about it|Avoid fake weaknesses like 'I work too hard'"),
("a05","airline","How do you handle criticism from a senior pilot?","Listen, don't argue in the moment|Take what's useful, ask questions to understand|Debrief calmly if you disagree|Example from training"),
("a06","airline","What do you know about our fleet and where we fly?","Main aircraft type(s) and roughly how many|Main bases and a few key routes, domestic and international|Recent news (new aircraft, new routes)|Shows you prepared"),
("a07","airline","Why should we choose you over the other candidates?","Two or three specific qualities with evidence|Safety mindset, teamwork, adaptability|Don't put others down"),
("a08","airline","How do you balance this job with the irregular hours?","Understand rosters, nights and time away|Good rest habits, fitness and a support system|Shows you've thought it through"),
("a09","airline","What does 'safety culture' mean to you?","Everyone reports mistakes and hazards without fear (just culture)|Following SOPs even when no one is watching|Learning from incidents|An example of you reporting or speaking up"),
("a10","airline","Tell us about a time you worked in a team that wasn't getting along.","STAR format|What you did to help: listening, clarifying roles, staying calm|Result and what you learned"),
("a11","airline","How do you keep your knowledge current while waiting for a job?","Revising technical subjects and SOPs|Simulator sessions or type-rating prep|Reading safety reports and aviation news|Keeping licence and medical valid"),
("a12","airline","Do you have any questions for us?","Always have one or two ready|Ask about training, the first year on the line, or culture|Avoid asking about pay in the first interview"),
]
